title: Source Control
files:
  - em.prog:em.examples.basic/Ex02_BlinkerP.em.ts
  - .assets/img-100-04-01.png

steps:
  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 12
    focus: [1, 1]
    acts: ["$reveal", "$build"]
    text: |

      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;Experimenting with EM&bull;Script}

      Going forward, we'll regularly encourage you to not only explore **EMS** content but *modify* it as well.

      <div class="em-info">

      Don't you want to change that constant on line {[bm,A]} ??

      </div>

      This tour therefore recommends some "best practices" using **Git** source control to sandbox your own work.

      Once in place, you can start experimenting 🧪&thinsp;&hellip;


  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |

      {[ht,rebase]&thinsp;Source Control &ndash; A Branch for Work}

      Initially running the <code>git clone&thinsp;&hellip;</code> command from your shell has defined a relation between a pair of repos:

      {[DC,1]} a *remote* `emporium` repo housed at **GitHub**<br>
      {[DC,2]} a *local* `emporium` repo residing on your PC

      Repo {[dc,2]} typically refers to repo {[dc,1]} as its `origin` &ndash; with its local `main` branch automatically tracking `origin:main`.

      <div class="em-info">

      `origin:main` holds the latest *stable* `emporium` release.

      </div>

      From here, you'll enter one essential command at the shell in&shy;side the `~/path/to/emporium` folder on your PC:

      ```bash
          git switch -c work
      ```

      Voilà &ndash; you've created {[dc,3]} a local sandbox name `work`&thinsp;&hellip;

  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,rebase]&thinsp;Source Control &ndash; Commit or Discard}

      You can always identify the current branch in local repo {[dc,2]} by entering `git status` at the command-line.

      <div class="em-info">

      Alternatively, use the [Source Control](https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/sourcecontrol/overview) features already inte&shy;grated with **VS Code** for a richer user experience.

      </div>

      On the `work` branch, you can add&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;edit&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;remove indi&shy;vid&shy;ual **EMS** source files &ndash; plus create *new* <b>Packages&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;Buckets</b>.

      The **Source Control** view found on the **VS Code Primary Sidebar** clearly summarizes changes you've made.

      From here, you can elect to either **commit** or **discard** any changes as appropriate.

      <div class="em-info">

      Just remember &ndash; always "work" on your `work` branch&thinsp;&hellip;

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,rebase]&thinsp;Source Control &ndash; Pull&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;Merge}

      In steady state, `main` in {[dc,2]} tracks `origin:main` in {[dc,1]}; and local `work` {[dc,3]} holds commits you wish to retain.

      Eventually, `origin:main` grows to include the next stable release of the **EM&bull;porium** environment at its tip.

      Use the following `git` commands to first sync local `main` and then merge {[dc,4]} the new release into `work`:

      ```bash
          git switch main; git pull
          git switch work; git merge main
      ```

      Review the changes back in the **Source Control** view, re&shy;solve any source file conflicts, and then commit to `work`.

      <div class="em-info">

      ⚠️ <u><i>Always</i></u> relaunch the environment with your `emp` shell alias when moving to a new **EM&bull;porium** release.

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |

      {[ht,rebase]&thinsp;Source Control &ndash; Contributing Content}

  - cmds:
    text: |

      {[ht,$extra]&thinsp;Extra Credit}

  - cmds:
    text: |

      {[ht,$steps]&thinsp;Next Steps}

      {[hc]}
